    <div id="sC-component-footnotes" class="ts-footnote-2">
<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> I would like to thank Linsey Molloy, Matt Raskin, and  Patricia Zobel for their assistance in preparing these remarks, Kathleena  Inchoco and Linmei Amaya for their research support, and colleagues in the  Federal Reserve System for valuable comments and suggestions. </p>
  <p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> The Federal Reserve also took supervisory and regulatory measures to respond to  the pandemic and associated crisis, including the reduction of reserve  requirement ratios to zero, the temporary exclusion of reserves and Treasuries  from the Supplementary Leverage Ratio (SLR), and the increased availability of  daylight credit.</p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220126c.htm">Principles for Reducing the Size of the Federal  Reserve&rsquo;s Balance Sheet</a>.</p>

<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> For additional discussion of Treasury market  intermediation during the onset of the pandemic, see Logan, Lorie, 2020, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log201023">Treasury Market Liquidity and Early Lessons from the Pandemic  Shock</a>,&rdquo; remarks at Brookings-Chicago  Booth Task Force on Financial Stability meeting, October 23. </p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> While <em>announcements</em> of central bank actions can also reduce liquidity  demand and encourage a return to normal investing and market-making activity,  the most immediate liquidity needs are unlikely to be resolved by announcements  alone and require active operations to address them.  For additional discussion, see Logan  (2020).  For additional discussion of the  impact of the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s asset purchases during the onset of the  pandemic and related crisis, see Vissing-Jorgensen, Annette, 2021, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w29128/w29128.pdf">The Treasury Market in Spring 2020 and the Response of  the Federal Reserve</a>,&rdquo; Working Paper  29128, National Bureau of Economic Research, August; Fleming, Michael, Haoyang  Liu, Rich Podjasek, and Jake Schurmeier, 2021, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/medialibrary/media/research/staff_reports/sr998.pdf">The Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Market Functioning Purchases</a>,&rdquo; Federal Reserve Bank of New York <em>Staff Reports</em>,  No. 998, December; and Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, 2020,  &ldquo;<a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/publications/files/financial-stability-report-20201109.pdf">Financial Stability Report</a>,&rdquo; November 16.</p>

<p> <a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> A variety of market functioning metrics informed the Desk&rsquo;s decision to reduce  purchases during this period.  The Desk  routinely monitors direct measures of liquidity and relative value, as well as  indirect measures of trading pressures and positioning preferences in the  Federal Reserve&rsquo;s open market operations.   For more discussion of the asset purchases to support market  functioning, see Logan, Lorie, 2020, &ldquo;<a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2020/log200715">The  Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Market Functioning Purchases: From Supporting to Sustaining</a>,&rdquo;  remarks at SIFMA Webinar, July 15. </p>

<p> <a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> There were sectors of the curve that the Desk refrained from  purchasing. For example, the Desk generally has avoided purchasing bills in  this program, both to avoid increasing scarcity value in these securities  arising from strong investor demand and because bill purchases have relatively  little impact on financial conditions.   Floating-rate notes (FRNs) have also not been purchased in operations  since March 2020 due to the limited impact these purchases would have on  financial conditions.</p>
<p> <a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> For additional discussion of measures to enhance U.S. Treasury market liquidity  and resiliency, see:&nbsp;&ldquo;<a href="https://home.treasury.gov/system/files/136/IAWG-Treasury-Report.pdf">Recent  Disruptions and Potential Reforms in the US Treasury Market: A Staff Progress  Report</a>,&rdquo; November 8, 2021.</p>
 <p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup>  See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/review-of-monetary-policy-strategy-tools-and-communications-statement-on-longer-run-goals-monetary-policy-strategy.htm">2020  Statement on Longer-Run Goals and Monetary Policy Strategy</a>.</p>
 <p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup>  See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220126a.htm">Federal  Reserve issues FOMC statement</a>, January 26, 2022,<a name="OLE_LINK15"> and </a><a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220126c.htm">Principles for Reducing the Size of the Federal  Reserve's Balance Sheet</a>.</p>

<p> <a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> The FOMC directs the Desk to reinvest all principal payments from the Federal  Reserve&rsquo;s holdings of Treasuries, agency debt and agency MBS; principal  payments from holdings of agency CMBS are not reinvested.</p>
  <p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20220126c.htm">Principles for Reducing the Size of the Federal  Reserve's Balance Sheet</a>. </p>
  <p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20220126.pdf">Minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee,  January 25–26, 2022</a>. </p>
  <p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> The shorter WAM of SOMA  Treasury holdings also reflects the effect of U.S. Treasury&rsquo;s debt issuance  decisions, and that for most of the program purchases have been proportional to  outstanding Treasury coupons. </p>


<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup>  Maturities of SOMA Treasury securities spike in the  mid-month of each quarter, reflecting Treasury issuance patterns and SOMA purchases  of 10- and 30-year Treasury securities, including those that were purchased  during the MEP and LSAP3.</p>
  <p><a name="footnote16"></a><sup>16</sup> In addition to the uncertainty associated with the level of interest rates,  estimates of agency MBS paydowns can vary substantially across paydown models.</p>
  <p><a name="footnote17"></a><sup>17</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20211215.pdf">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee, December 14-15, 2021</a>.</p>

<p><a name="footnote18"></a><sup>18</sup>  See the <a name="OLE_LINK69"></a><a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/sfos/aug-2019-senior-financial-officer-survey.htm">August 2019  Senior Financial Officer Survey</a>.</p>
  <p><a name="footnote19"></a><sup>19</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20211215.pdf">Minutes of the Federal Open Market Committee,  December 14-15, 2021</a>.<a name="OLE_LINK22"></a></p>
<p><a name="footnote20"></a><sup>20</sup>  See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20220126.pdf">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee, January 25–26, 2022</a>.</p>

<p><a name="footnote21"></a><sup>21</sup>  The most recent Senior Financial Officer Survey asked respondents whether they expect their bank to take actions to reduce or increase the  level or growth in its reserve balances in the next six months. About 40  percent of respondents (a vast majority of which were domestic banks) reported  that they expect their bank to take actions to reduce the level or growth in  its reserve balances.  Over 50 percent of  the respondents reported that they expect their bank to not take specific  actions to adjust the level or growth in its reserve balances, while the  remaining reported that they expect their bank to take actions to increase the  level or growth of its reserve balances.   For further information about banks&rsquo; reserve balance management and  strategies, see the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/sfos/november-2021-senior-financial-officer-survey.htm">November 2021 Senior Financial Officer Survey</a>. </p>

    </div>
         

</div>

